<!DOCTYPE html>
{% assign resolved_dir = dir | default: "auto" %}
<html lang="{{locale}}" dir="{{resolved_dir}}">
  <head>
    {%- auth0:head -%}
    <title>{{ prompt.screen.texts.pageTitle }}</title>
    <style>
      /* Fixes Auth0's widget cannot express through the branding editor.
         Auth0's page-template docs warn that generated class names change on
         every widget build, so prefer their `_`-prefixed classes and `data-`
         attributes, which are semantic, over the hashed `c...` ones. Re-check
         the live login screen after Auth0 releases. Nothing here is
         load-bearing: if a selector stops matching, the screen still works and
         only reverts to Auth0's default appearance. */

      /* Social buttons render at 16px while the passkey and submit buttons are
         14px, so the provider list looks mismatched. Note this is true on the
         production tenant but not on dev, which is on a different build. */
      [data-action-button-secondary='true'] { font-size: 0.875rem !important; }

      /* Auth0 renders every button at weight 400. The console's `.button` base
         class is font-medium, so match it. The branding editor's bold toggles
         are binary (400 or 700) and cannot express 500. The label sits in a
         span that carries its own weight, so the rule has to reach
         descendants; setting it on the button alone does nothing. */
      ._prompt-box-outer button,
      ._prompt-box-outer button * { font-weight: 500 !important; }

      /* The passkey button repeats the 24px margin its container already
         applies, and the container's own margin then leaves ~50px of dead
         space above the provider group. Zero both; ~26px of inherited spacing
         remains, which is the gap we actually want. */
      ._passkey-button-container { margin-top: 0 !important; }
      ._passkey-button-container .passkey-begin-button { margin-top: 0 !important; }

      /* Auth0 force-uppercases the separator in CSS, so the `separatorText`
         custom-text value ("or") renders as "OR" regardless of what it is set to. */
      ._prompt-box-outer * { text-transform: none !important; }

      /* Required marker on a form where every field is required. Already
         aria-hidden, so hiding it costs nothing for assistive tech. */
      label .required { display: none; }

      /* Auth0 leaves 16px below the h1, which reads loose against the
         description line that follows it. Tighten to 8px so title and
         description group as one unit. Applies to every screen that has a
         description; the passkey-enrolment block below overrides this to 0,
         since those titles stand alone with no description under them. */
      ._prompt-box-outer h1 { margin-bottom: 8px !important; }

      /* The email-verified result draws its status icon as an 88px masked span
         (`.success-lock`), which dominates an otherwise calm card. Shrink the
         box to 48px so it reads as an accent above the message. The mask is an
         84px SVG, so size it to `contain` or it clips instead of scaling. The
         class is semantic and appears only on this screen, so no screen guard
         is needed: it matches nothing elsewhere. */
      .success-lock {
        width: 48px !important;
        height: 48px !important;
        -webkit-mask-size: contain !important;
        mask-size: contain !important;
      }

      /* Passkey enrolment: the button renders above the benefits it is meant to
         follow. The flex container holds the benefits form and a wrapper div
         around the button, so `order` must go on the wrapper, not the button:
         an order on the button itself is inert because its own parent is a
         block. `:has()` reaches the wrapper without using its hashed class.
         The alternate actions default to order:4, so they stay last. */
      ._form-passkey-enrollment { order: 1 !important; }
      ._prompt-box-outer div:has(> .passkey-begin-button) { order: 2 !important; }

      /* Benefit list. The stock grey icons are off-brand and cannot be swapped
         through CSS, so drop them and collapse the grid to a single column.
         The list sits on a borderless zinc-50 plate with hairline dividers,
         the same treatment the console uses for reference content. Dividers
         replace the gaps rather than adding to them: two separation devices
         for three rows reads airy and disconnected. */
      .ulp-passkey-benefits {
        background: #fafafa !important;
        border-radius: 8px !important;
        padding: 16px !important;
        gap: 0 !important;
        margin-bottom: 0 !important;
      }
      .ulp-passkey-benefit {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;
        align-items: start !important;
        padding: 12px 0 !important;
        border-top: 1px solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05) !important;
      }
      .ulp-passkey-benefit:first-child { border-top: 0 !important; padding-top: 0 !important; }
      .ulp-passkey-benefit:last-child { padding-bottom: 0 !important; }
      .ulp-passkey-benefit > *:not(.ulp-passkey-benefit-content) { display: none !important; }
      .ulp-passkey-benefit-content { gap: 2px !important; }
      .ulp-passkey-benefit-heading {
        font-size: 0.875rem !important;
        font-weight: 500 !important;
        line-height: 1.4 !important;
        margin: 0 !important;
      }
      .ulp-passkey-benefit-content > *:not(.ulp-passkey-benefit-heading) {
        font-size: 0.875rem !important;
        line-height: 1.5 !important;
        margin: 0 !important;
      }

      /* A wrapper hard-codes min-height: 540px, so short screens like
         reset-password and the email-verification result pad themselves out
         with dead space to match the tall ones, and their vertically centred
         content floats in the middle of it. Which node carries the height
         varies by screen: the login flow puts it on the inner wrapper, the
         verification result on the card itself. Zero both so the card always
         hugs its content. Trade-off: the card now changes height between steps
         of a flow rather than staying fixed. */
      ._prompt-box-outer > div,
      ._prompt-box-outer > div > div { min-height: 0 !important; }

      /* Auth0's widget shadow is 0 12px 40px at 12% black, which floats the
         card well off the page. Softer and layered, closer to the console. */
      ._prompt-box-outer > div {
        box-shadow:
          0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.04),
          0 8px 24px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06) !important;
      }
    </style>
    {% if prompt.screen.name == 'passkey-enrollment' or prompt.screen.name == 'passkey-enrollment-local' %}
    <style>
      /* On the enrolment screens the h1 sits 40px above the content: 16px of
         its own margin plus 24px structural. Zeroing the margin lands on ~24px,
         matching the login screen. Scoped by screen name because other titles
         are followed by a description that needs the 16px. */
      ._prompt-box-outer h1 { margin-bottom: 0 !important; }

      /* Vertical rhythm around the benefits plate. 30px, not 24px, and this is
         deliberate: the gap above the plate is 24px measured, but its neighbour
         is a text line box whose bottom sits several pixels below the last
         visible ink, so it reads as roughly 30px. The gap below neighbours a
         solid button edge, where measured and perceived are the same. Equal
         numbers here look unequal; do not "correct" this to 24.

         Both alternate actions keep Auth0's 16px, which suits them as text
         links; a tighter value only made sense while the opt-out was a button. */
      .passkey-begin-button { margin-top: 30px !important; }

    </style>
    {% endif %}
  </head>
  <body class="_widget-auto-layout">
    {%- auth0:widget -%}
  </body>
</html>
